We develop an adaptive proximal alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMM) for linearly constrained composite optimization with a smooth, weakly convex component and a convex, block-separable nonsmooth component with compact domain. The method adapts to problem parameters, including smoothness and weak convexity constants, and permits inexact solutions of its block subproblems.

Our analysis establishes iteration complexity guarantees for finding approximate first-order stationary points without rank assumptions on the constraint matrices. These guarantees match the best available bounds for proximal ADMM methods, while numerical experiments demonstrate the computational benefits of the adaptive approach.

